---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import reads from '../data/reads.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// A small "now" block, built from what is already on the site so it
// never needs updating by hand. One item per row, each linking to its
// place on this site. Rows with nothing in them are dropped.
const [book] = reads.current; // already newest-added first
const [quest] = (
  await getCollection('sidequests', ({ data }) => !data.draft && data.status === 'active')
).sort((a, b) => b.data.started.valueOf() - a.data.started.valueOf());

const [latest] = (await getCollection('essays', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf()
);

const now = [
  book && { label: 'Reading', title: book.title, href: `${base}/reads#book-${book.id}` },
  quest && { label: 'Side quest', title: quest.data.title, href: `${base}/sidequests/${quest.id}` },
  latest && { label: 'Latest essay', title: latest.data.title, href: `${base}/essays/${latest.id}` },
].filter((row) => !!row);
---

<Base title="Moving Parts" backdrop="full">
  <h1>Moving Parts</h1>

  <p class="lede">Field notes from taking things apart.</p>

  <p>
    Over the coming decade, as AI gets more capable, I believe we’ll have solutions faster than we can put them to
    good use. <strong>The hard part shifts from finding solutions to applying them gracefully.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    What I want to understand is how. At minimum, that takes three things:
  </p>

  <ul class="three">
    <li><span class="label">What’s right:</span> AI safety, alignment and policies </li>
    <li>
      <span class="label">What’s feasible:</span> guardrails, grounded in computational
      complexity, for choosing and building solutions at scale
    </li>
    <li><span class="label">What’s supportable:</span> the physical infrastructure to run them</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    I’m Moksh, a software engineer based in Bengaluru, India. I take systems
    apart to understand them.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site is where I work through these ideas in the open. Start with
    the <a href={`${base}/essays`}>essays</a>, or see what I’m building
    under <a href={`${base}/sidequests`}>Side Quests</a>.
  </p>

  {now.length > 0 && (
    <dl class="now">
      {now.map(({ label, title, href }) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{label}</dt>
          <dd><a href={href}>{title}</a></dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  )}

  <style>
    .lede {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      line-height: 1.5;
    }

    .three {
      padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    }

    .three li {
      margin-block: 0.4rem;
    }

    .three .label {
      font-style: italic;
    }

    .now {
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.4rem;
      margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
    }

    .now > div {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .now dt {
      color: var(--text-muted);
    }

    .now dd {
      margin: 0;
    }

    .now a {
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .now a:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
